    "body": "@annevk \r\n\r\n> > It also breaks having top-level switch be, functionally speaking, a simple switch on the state of the dir attribute.\r\n>\r\n> Doesn't the bdi element break this?\r\n\r\nTechnically yes, if you go strictly by \"what is the value of the dir attribute in the attribute OM\"; but functionally not really. The top-level switch is: dir=ltr, dir=rtl, dir=auto, dir inherited.\r\n\r\nUnlike every other element, BDI defaults its dir value to auto (rather than having a distinct “missing, get it from somewhere else” state). So it gets grouped with the dir=auto case, alongside `span[dir=auto]` for which it is basically just an alias.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
